cbs evening news anchor and managing  editor that's nora o'donnell's in  mayfield where the tornado damage is so  extensive people say you can't even  believe what you're watching nora good  morning i know you were there and you  were seeing it firsthand  yeah gail where i'm standing i mean it  just goes on for block after block just  houses i mean just totally destroyed  it's just  it's difficult to fathom how much pain  there is to among many of these families  one of the many stories to be told here  in mayfield involves a minister who  narrowly escapes the tornado the  reverend joey reed survived by taking  shelter with his wife in the basement  closet of mayfield first united  methodist church when they emerged they  found that much of the building had  crumbled around them and here's what the  church looked like before and after the  tornado hit pastor reid went back with  our camera crew to see the damage  yesterday and here's what he had to say  [Music]  we came out and i realized that i was  looking up  to see the  extent of the damage to the church i was  looking at the sky  south wall here collapsed inward and the  ceiling came in  the devastation around us is incredible  all of that was part of a molar organ  that was historic  you can see some of the the ceiling of  the portico here you can see where  that's falling in and the brickwork is  all over the steps  thanks be to god the parts of the  building that came down didn't come down  on us  i realized that it might be the last few  moments of my life on this earth and i  was very glad to be with my wife i know  her prayer and mine  was that we might be spared i was afraid  for my children what would happen to  them and how they would respond to this  my daughter's going to be married in  march  and i'm supposed to do the service and i  was thinking of that  quite a bit as the as the tornado was  bearing down on us  all i care about is the fact that the  most valuable possessions in my life my  wife's life my children they're all safe  and everything else is replaceable  the reverend joey reed is with us now  and  reverend thank you and i mean  the people i've talked to too amidst all  this despair feel blessed that they're  alive  it's  it's one of the qualities of living in  mayfield kentucky is to see the  resilience of the the populace  we had a service of worship yesterday  our bishop was in town our district  superintendent the united methodist  church is connectional and one of my  colleagues very quickly offered us a  place to come and worship  and i offered to make that worship  service something after his he said no  let's worship together and as we came  together we're in the season of advent  and because advent  contains those those four major topics  of hope and peace this sunday was love  the third i'm sorry this sunday was joy  the third sunday of advent was already  planned  and  we realized that we were going to have  to do some tweaking but when we looked  at the service we didn't have to change  very much how did you give a sermon  about joy amidst all this  there was one surviving bulletin that my  administrative assistant at the church  had held on to the rest are at the  bottom of that pile of rubble that you  saw  on the back of that bulletin it said a  sermon synopsis basically joy is often  mistaken for happiness but happiness can  change by a turn of events  joy is something that abides and that's  what we're holding on to  you've also talked about how you're  counseling people you know one of the  families i talked to said they have no  savings they are on monthly disability  they have nowhere to go  how do you counsel them what do you tell  them about keeping the faith  well the first thing that we do is we  find those needs and we attempt to meet  those needs yesterday in that service of  worship our district disaster relief  coordinator bill carr was here bill is  one of those guys who comes in and  assesses the situation and sends groups  to take care of those immediate needs  moving heavy equipment into place and  he's all about trying to find those  bright spots how can we help how can we  make a positive difference we also had  our conference disaster relief  coordinator whose primary job is to  interface with fema the reverend robert  craig has been working with disaster  relief for quite some time and as a  result knows how to make sure that those  dollars are flowing to the right places  and spiritually spiritually that's where  the local pastors come in like myself we  are trying to help people understand  that as they move from their place of  suffering into their discipleship their  servant leadership it's it's we for a  long time talked about how discipleship  is forged in a crucible of suffering and  after that suffering is acknowledged and  that lamentation a word that we don't  talk about very much because it's an old  testament word for so many people after  we've mourned our losses and started to  let go of the things that mean so much  to us we can move into a bright future  where we're helping our neighbors and  that's what mayfield kentucky is about  there is so much suffering and  you know you just described it you're  supposed to be marrying your daughter  in the spring in in march and that's  about all i could think about in that  closet when the tornado was going over  please lord let me make it so that i can  see my little girl married  and  not going to be in the same church but  no the good news is where the service is  planned for elsewhere she's still living  in jackson tennessee  but  we've lost the sanctuary where so many  people have seen  that very event take place marriages  baptisms we've conducted funerals and  laid to rest so many of our members that  building was the repository of our  memories and we have to remember that  those memories still belong to us they  cannot be taken from us by something  even as devastating as this tornado  well reverend it's been lovely to speak  with you this morning and i know so many  people will depend on you in these  coming years ahead thank you for the  chance to tell these stories of  resilience i appreciate it very much  thank you pastor joey reed  now let's go back to you interesting  what he said about  so much of your faith forged in the  crucible of suffering  nate  nora thank you
 

